cartridge "DOGCATCHER/B.BLACKMER/1986"

' DOGCATCHER
' A ColecoVision game based on "Dogcatcher" by Bob Blackmer, Ahoy!
' September 1986 (Commodore 64 machine language at $C000, 15 blocks; author
' from the program's own bottom line, "DOGCATCHER BY BOB BLACKMER (C) 1986",
' and the issue's contents page). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made
' by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/dogcatcher.dis), the game is built for the
' ColecoVision: a cut-away brick tenement at night, wooden floors with
' ladders down the holes, stray dogs and a dogcatcher as smooth sprites,
' a pound truck at the kerb, sound effects.
'
' The game: four strays roam the five storeys. Hold the button to put your
' net out in front of you and let a dog walk into it - the net must face
' the dog. A dog that touches you any other way (walking, climbing, the
' net the wrong way, a dog already in the net, two at once) bites. Carry
' each dog down the ladders to the truck at the right of the street. When
' all four are in the truck it drives off, comes back, and the strays are
' loose again, a little faster.
'
' From the original (orig/): six standing heights (five floors and the
' street) 32 C64 pixels apart, holes in the middle of floors 1, 3 and 5 and
' at both sides of floors 2 and 4; the catcher walks 2 pixels a step, only
' when standing on a floor, and climbs 2 pixels a step only through a hole
' (and on between floors); the street stops him at the truck; the strays
' go in two pairs, one dog of each pair always walking right and the other
' always left, on the original's two fixed courses (pair one: in to the
' middle of floor 1, down, out to the edges of floor 2, again on floors 3
' and 4; pair two: in to the side holes of floor 2, down, in to the middle
' of floor 3, down, out on floor 4, across floor 5), a pixel a step,
' dropping through the holes a pixel a step; a caught dog is gone until
' the round ends; touching a dog: caught if the net is out toward it (a dog
' walking right is caught by a net facing left), else a bite; a bite puts
' the catcher back on the street; 9 lives and a bite with none left ends
' the game; 100 points a dog, the dog count, the level; after four dogs the
' truck drives off and back while everything else waits, and the steps
' come about 2% faster each level (the original's delay loop, 25 to 1:
' SpdT).
'
' Liberties: the field is the ColecoVision's 28 columns (the C64 had 30)
' and storeys 24 pixels apart (32); the courses keep their shapes and
' lengths in steps (528 steps round for both pairs, as in the original);
' the original's speed at its fastest stays from level 25 on; a dog in the
' net when you are bitten runs free again (the original let you keep it
' and deliver it at once from the street); touching is a box test (the
' C64 used its sprite collision register); the net stays out as long as
' the button is held while standing (as on the C64, where holding the
' trigger swung it every pass), a dog in the net cannot be dropped;
' ladders drawn in the holes; the C64's looping tune during play is
' replaced by sound effects (barks, a yelp, the net, the cage door, the
' horn); a lives row of dog tags instead of LIVES 000009; the bite is a
' still pause with the catcher turned red (the C64 flashed every sprite's
' colour many times a second); GAME OVER then the title (the C64 waited
' for the trigger to start again). Standing still is safe only on the
' street and scores nothing; a dog can only be caught with the net, and
' only delivered by walking the ladders, so nothing is won by waiting.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick walks left and right on a floor
' and climbs up and down through the holes; hold a side button to put the
' net out. On the title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

